# Two-part workaround for the intermittent AYN Odin3 gamepad freeze after wake:
#
#   1. `rsinput` is listed in /usr/config/modules.bad so the sleep hook unloads
#      it pre-suspend. This clears the qcom-geni serial port of any active
#      client and prevents qcom_geni_serial_suspend from WARN'ing — verified
#      2026-05-31 that once that WARN fires the bus is wedged at a level no
#      userspace recovery (modprobe / sysfs unbind) can reset; only a reboot
#      does. So prevention has to be airtight.
#
#   2. THIS quirk restarts InputPlumber unconditionally after `modules start`
#      has reloaded rsinput. Without an explicit restart, IP's evdev discovery
#      thread races with the unload/reload sequence: on roughly every other
#      wake it sees ENODEV on its existing fd but fails to tear down the stale
#      CompositeDevice, so it never picks up the freshly re-probed event4 and
#      the controller appears dead until the *next* suspend/wake cycle finally
#      clears the state (logs verified 2026-05-31 — see commit message).
#
# Cost: a visible "controller disconnected → connected" toast at every wake.
# This is the deliberate trade-off chosen by the user over the freeze.

# Guarantee the stick calibration is in the module BEFORE this restart:
# InputPlumber caches the declared absinfo at attach, so restarting against
# the driver's default +/-1024 would leave every stick short until the next
# restart. The udev module-add rule usually wins this race; make it certain.
# (rsinput-cal-apply itself never restarts IP - this hook owns the wake
# restart, exactly one per resume.)
[ -x /run/rsinput/rsinput-cal-apply ] && /run/rsinput/rsinput-cal-apply

systemctl restart inputplumber
